# The shared conformance vectors use LF line endings by their schema (conformance/vectors/SCHEMA.md,
# section 1), and the validator refuses a line that ends in CR. A checkout that converted them to
# CRLF - Git for Windows' default, core.autocrlf=true - would make every vector file unreadable, and
# the generator's test compares the committed corpus with what it writes byte for byte. So the corpus
# and its generator's inputs are checked out with LF everywhere.
conformance/** text eol=lf
scripts/conformance/** text eol=lf

# The shared JavaScript libraries (Broiler.ECMAScript/, milestone SJL-2.10) are stored with LF line
# endings everywhere: code may move between this repository and Broiler.VM, whose rule J6 and whose
# assurance fingerprints read the text as stored, and the shared rule SL10 refuses a CR. The engine's
# own folders keep whatever they have; some of them are CRLF or mixed today.
Broiler.ECMAScript/** text eol=lf
Broiler.ECMAScript.slnf text eol=lf
# Rule SL10's witness for CRLF line endings must keep its CRs, so it alone is stored as-is.
Broiler.ECMAScript/Broiler.ECMAScript.Architecture.Tests/witnesses/SL10-crlf-line-endings.cs.witness -text

# THE TIME DATA HOME'S PINNED INPUTS AND BINARY TABLES ARE BINARY (milestone SJL-8.2). The archives are
# held by their SHA-256 in tzdb.pin, calendars.pin and cldr.pin, byte-for-byte copies of Broiler.VM's, and
# the generated tables by rule SL7 and their manifest. The folder's `text eol=lf` above would let a checkout
# filter rewrite a byte under a recorded hash, so these lines, which come after it, take it back. The CLDR
# file and its licence are LF text today and are declared binary as Broiler.VM declares its copies. The
# pins, the READMEs, the text tables and the manifest stay `text eol=lf`: the tests read them byte for
# byte, and LF is what the generator writes and what the pins' digests were taken over.
Broiler.ECMAScript/data/time/tzdb/*.tar.gz binary
Broiler.ECMAScript/data/time/calendars/*.crate binary
Broiler.ECMAScript/data/time/cldr/cldr-48.2.0/** binary
Broiler.ECMAScript/data/time/cldr/cldr-LICENSE.txt binary
Broiler.ECMAScript/data/time/generated/*.bin binary

# THE REFERENCE DATASETS ARE HELD BY THEIR DIGESTS (milestone SJL-8.4). Bundle sjl-8-001's rule, part C, takes
# Broiler.VM's two programs, their retained answers and their divergences "byte for byte", and "a copy whose
# digest differs is not the population"; datasets.pin records each copy's SHA-256. They are LF text today, so
# the folder's `text eol=lf` above changes none of them, but it would strip a CR on commit, so these lines,
# which come after it, take it back: no filter touches the copies. They stay `-text` rather than binary
# so that a diff of one still reads. The pin and the README stay `text eol=lf`. Milestone SJL-10.2 added the
# last three, temporal-calendars.js's dataset, which bundle sjl-10-001's rule, part C, takes the same way.
conformance/datasets/time/timezones.js -text
conformance/datasets/time/timezones.icu-77.1.txt -text
conformance/datasets/time/timezones.divergences.txt -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal.js -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal.polyfill-e8cc03fc.txt -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal.divergences.txt -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal-calendars.js -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal-calendars.polyfill-e8cc03fc.txt -text
conformance/datasets/time/temporal-calendars.divergences.txt -text
# The runs of those datasets that bundle sjl-8-001 retains are kept as the harness wrote them: each run's
# summary.json records its answers' SHA-256, and its line reports are read back by the harness's compare.
docs/evidence/sjl-8-001/datasets/** -text
# And bundle sjl-10-001's, from milestone SJL-10.2, the same way.
docs/evidence/sjl-10-001/datasets/** -text

# CI job logs retained in an evidence bundle are kept byte for byte as GitHub Actions served them;
# the Windows jobs' logs have CRLF line endings.
docs/evidence/*/ci/*.log -text

# THE PINNED UNICODE ARCHIVE IS BINARY (milestone SJL-7.5, decision SJL-D-6 (c)). Its files are held by
# their SHA-256 in data/unicode/pins/unicode.pin, a byte-for-byte copy of Broiler.Unicode's, and a
# checkout filter that rewrote one line ending would move a hash on one platform. The pin and the
# archive's README stay text: nothing hashes them. These lines are Broiler.Unicode's own.
data/unicode/pins/ucd-17.0.0/** binary
data/unicode/pins/emoji-17.0.0/** binary
data/unicode/pins/ecma262/** binary
data/unicode/pins/unicode-LICENSE.txt binary
data/unicode/pins/unicode.pin text eol=lf
data/unicode/pins/README.md text eol=lf
data/unicode/pins/ecma262/ECMA-alternative-copyright-notice.txt text eol=lf
# The tables generated from that archive are compared with the generator's output byte for byte, and
# the generator writes LF line endings.
Broiler.JS/Broiler.JavaScript.Runtime/Unicode/JSUnicodeTableData.g.cs text eol=lf
